## Open, smaller

### Clause variables in a definition block take the FIRST PARAM'S SORT

Found while surveying the corpus for definition migrations (`7dff5a4`). A free
variable a definition's declaration does not bind takes the sort of the first
param. Right by luck for recursive clauses (`k` in `add(succ(k), b)` is a `Nat`
like `a`), wrong for the membership style:

    func union(a: Set, b: Set) => Set:
      member(x, union(a, b)) iff (member(x, a) or member(x, b))
    -- error: expected sort 'Element', got 'Set'   (at `x`; re-probed 2026-09-27)

The fix is to take the sort from USE (`member`'s first argument pins
`x : Element`), which subsumes the first-param heuristic. Blocks TIER 2 of
DEFINITION-MIGRATION-CANDIDATES. Hit again 2026-09-26 writing `cycle`:

    func cycle(s: Points, k: Nat) => Fn:
      cycle(s, ZERO) = identityFn;
      cycle(s, succ(k)) = compose(transposition(at(s, ZERO), at(s, k)), cycle(s, k))
    -- error: expected sort 'Nat', got 'Points'   (at `k`)

Also why `std/peano/factorial.b4m` states n! as two axioms rather than a block.

### A bare constant on a clause's RIGHT-HAND side is read as a clause variable

Same probe, first clause: `cycle(s, ZERO) = identityFn` is rejected with
"'identityFn' shadows a declaration; choose a fresh name" — the RHS constant is
taken for a fresh clause variable. (`ZERO` in the HEAD is accepted as a pattern.)
Resolution should look identifiers up before minting clause variables. Until
both are fixed, `std/permutation/cycle.b4m` states its two clauses as plain
axioms (`cycleZero`, `cycleSucc`). Re-probed 2026-09-27: still minted — the RHS
`identityFn` now reports "expected sort 'Fn', got 'Nat'" (a fresh clause variable
of the first param's sort, i.e. the bug above compounding this one).

### Instantiating a schema by QUALIFIED name resolves its parameter sort in the wrong scope

    [using instantiation a.tauto((fun g: Fn => g = identityFn))]
    -- error: unknown sort 'Fn'   AT THE SCHEMA'S DECLARATION in a.b4m

`a.b4m` has `sort Fn = function.Fn` and `theorem tauto(prop: Fn -> Prop)`; citing
it qualified from another file fails, aliasing first (`theorem tauto = a.tauto`)
passes. Every schema use across std aliases for this reason. Re-probed 2026-09-27
(three-file scratch: a / b_qualified / b_aliased).

### A theorem SCHEMA is checked only at a concrete instantiation

"'X' is a schema; it is checked at its instantiations" — a file of schemas alone
verifies NOTHING, so `std/permutation/listing.b4m` ends with a concrete instance
(`splittingOffTheIdentity`) to exercise the whole chain. Not a bug by the current
design, but a file-level trap worth a diagnostic (a schema no instance ever
exercises is as unverified as a hole). Contradicts the older memory note that
strict check verifies a schema at declaration.

